{"duration_api_ms":30976,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"454cd07a-6b25-43a4-91d7-ac3be5424d3e","total_cost_usd":0.23860399999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12505,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":4260,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1758},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12505,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4260,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12505},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6954,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.007044,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":4260,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12505,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.23156,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":1758,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"# The Carry Question: New Pack Reviews Are About How Weight Rides, Not Just How Much It Weighs\n\nThree recent pack reviews from separate publishers point to load stability and fit, not empty weight, as the variables worth watching.\n\nAs of October 15, 2026.\n\nAt Mammoth, everything is carried on foot, so a pack is a system you live in for days, not a bag you grab. A few ounces on the spec sheet matter less than whether the load wobbles at hour twelve, or whether your shoulders hold up when you're tired and wet. The three recent reviews I looked at lean that way. They are not Mammoth tests, and I'll say why below. But the direction is useful.\n\n## A frame redesign and a hunter's heavy-load test\n\nRokslide's review of the KUIU Icon 6 3500 comes from a backcountry hunter, which is a relevant perspective when the gear transfers to heavy carries. The reviewer reports that the Icon 6 was designed from scratch and is not backward compatible with earlier Icon packs and frames. [01-c1]\n\nKUIU's pitch is a stronger, more durable frame. Per the review, the new frame \"promises increased strength and durability.\" The same reviewer notes that earlier Icons got overwhelmed under really heavy loads, and that some hunters saw frames crack and break. The excerpts show no independent durability testing of the new frame, so the durability gain remains KUIU's claim as relayed by Rokslide. [01-c2]\n\nWhat the reviewer did report is a comfort result. He says the Icon 6 completely remedied the heavy-load fatigue he had with earlier Icons. He tested on 1.2-mile loops with 510 vertical feet gained and lost, carrying 28 lb of day gear, then added a 64 lb simulated elk quarter. [01-c3]\n\nOn weight, he measured the Icon 6 3500 at 5 lbs. 12.5 oz. with the accessories he would actually use, and describes that as in line with the Stone Glacier Sky 5900. [01-c4] The one weight comparison we have shows a redesigned pack landing near an existing competitor, not far from it.\n\n## Plate-carrying designs and a running vest\n\nRuck Authority's reviewer tested the GORUCK Rucker 5.0 for a week on trails in Wisconsin with a 20 lb plate. He did not use the optional hip belt. GORUCK sent him the bag, and he says the company didn't tell him what to say. Our editorial notes also list affiliate links for the review, though the excerpts I have don't show that disclosure. [01-c5]\n\nHis verdict on stability was strong. He said the pack felt stable, with no hot spots, chafing, numbness or lower-back discomfort. His traps were a little sore, and he thought a hip belt might help. [01-c6] That is a useful detail. Even in a favorable review, the shoulders took the load, and the missing hip belt was where he saw room for improvement.\n\nIn a separate video, he said the GORUCK GR1 worked at 20 lb without a chest strap. He also noted that plenty of experienced ruckers consider a sternum strap important as weight rises. For pure rucking, he concluded the Rucker 5.0 is the better choice. [01-c7] The two bags are built differently, and his preference came down to how each holds a load.\n\nAt the light end, Treeline Review (an independent site with an affiliate model) reported that the Black Diamond Distance 15 running vest bounces when heavily loaded, especially downhill, and has no hip belt. [01-c8] A 15-liter vest is built for running, so the bounce shows where the vest-style approach starts to break down, not a flaw in the product's intended use.\n\n## What I take from this\n\nTaken together, this suggests that load stability and fit under load, more than empty weight, are what separate these newly reviewed packs. [01-c9] Our read is that the Icon 6 landed near a competitor's weight, the Rucker was praised for staying still, and the Distance 15 was criticized for bouncing. None of the reviewers states this as a conclusion. It is our interpretation across three different products, and it describes a pattern in separate tests, not corroboration of any single pack.\n\n## Limits of the evidence\n\nNo supplied item tests gear at Mammoth or over multi-day team carries. The Icon 6 loops were 1.2 miles. The Rucker test was a week of trail use with 20 lb, far below what a Mammoth competitor will carry. Our brief records the Distance 15 reviewer as saying it isn't meant for 20+ liter overnight loads, though that passage isn't in the excerpts I have. Conditions like days of carry, rain and exhaustion come from the project brief, not from any of these reviews.\n\nOn sources, Rokslide is ad-supported and is a practitioner view from hunting. KUIU's own claims reach us only through that review. Ruck Authority received free product, and Treeline uses affiliate links. Both factors argue for treating praise as less than independent confirmation. No Mammoth organizer, past competitor or lab data is supplied. The official Mammoth page is not among the evidence, so I'm leaving required status unspecified. The packets are also excerpts, so omitted passages may hold qualifications or counterevidence.\n\n## Takeaway\n\nIf you are choosing or testing a pack for Mammoth, treat these reviews as a guide to what to check, not as a verdict. Load it to something near your real carry and walk it on rough ground, downhill included. Watch for wobble and for shoulder fatigue, and try it with and without the hip belt and sternum strap if the pack allows. Empty weights in this set look similar enough that how the pack carries may decide more than the scale does. A companion video is here: [VIDEO LINK]\n\n## Sources\n\n- 01-c1, 01-c2, 01-c3, 01-c4: Rokslide, \"KUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review,\" September 23, 2026. https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/ (practitioner reviewer; ad-supported site; KUIU claims relayed second-hand)\n- 01-c5: Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: My First Week on the Trail,\" September 24, 2026, at 0:04, 1:03 and 1:33. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVz1KOKvi-I (reviewer received product free)\n- 01-c6: Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: My First Week on the Trail,\" September 24, 2026, at 3:00. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVz1KOKvi-I (reviewer received product free)\n- 01-c7: Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK GR1 26L Review: An Everyday Bag That Can Ruck,\" October 2, 2026, at 2:05 and 3:06. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlW6o3cO2rk\n- 01-c8: Treeline Review, \"I tested the Black Diamond Distance 15 Backpack while running mountains across Colorado,\" October 1, 2026. https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/black-diamond-distance-15-backpack-review (independent; affiliate model)\n- 01-c9: Our inference, drawing on the Rokslide review above, the Ruck Authority Rucker 5.0 review at 3:00, and the Treeline Review piece above.\n\nThis article was drafted with AI assistance from cited sources and reviewed by a human editor.","ttft_ms":13673,"type":"result","duration_ms":30241,"uuid":"49bdbd00-163e-453d-9916-ceb68f11082f","ttft_stream_ms":1590,"time_to_request_ms":265,"first_content_frame_ms":1590,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
